{"term": "satyriasis", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "male_hypersexuality", "concept": "male_hypersexuality", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 10, "technical": 92, "recognition_pct_current": 20, "recognition_pct_peak": 28, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A clinical term for abnormally excessive or uncontrollable sexual desire in males.", "english_term": "satyriasis", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Largely superseded in modern psychiatry by 'compulsive sexual behavior disorder' (ICD-11) or 'hypersexual disorder'; the traditional female equivalent is nymphomania."}
{"term": "satyr", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "male_hypersexuality", "concept": "male_hypersexuality", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 20, "technical": 35, "recognition_pct_current": 55, "recognition_pct_peak": 62, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A lecherous man; derived from the half-human, half-goat creature of Greek mythology associated with sexual excess and drunkenness.", "english_term": "satyr", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Primary recognition is mythological; the sexual characterization sense is secondary and largely literary. Satyriasis derives from this root."}
{"term": "sex addict", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_addiction", "concept": "sexual_addiction", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 20, "technical": 40, "recognition_pct_current": 94, "recognition_pct_peak": 94, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A person with compulsive, difficult-to-control sexual thoughts or behaviors that cause distress or functional impairment.", "english_term": "sex addict", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Popularized by pop psychology and high-profile celebrity scandals (Tiger Woods, etc.); clinically contested — not an official DSM-5 diagnosis, though ICD-11 includes 'compulsive sexual behaviour disorder'."}
{"term": "erotomania", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "erotomania", "concept": "erotomania", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 8, "technical": 90, "recognition_pct_current": 16, "recognition_pct_peak": 20, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A psychiatric delusion in which a person believes a higher-status individual is secretly in love with them; historically also used as a synonym for hypersexuality.", "english_term": "erotomania", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Dominant modern sense is de Clérambault syndrome (the delusion), not sexual excess; the hypersexuality sense is archaic. The dual meaning causes consistent clinical confusion."}
{"term": "sex maniac", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_excess_crude", "concept": "sexual_excess_crude", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 45, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A person regarded as having an obsessive or uncontrollable sexual appetite; used pejoratively or humorously.", "english_term": "sex maniac", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Carries a stronger implication of predatory or deviant behavior than 'sex addict'; somewhat more common in British English. Often used hyperbolically."}
{"term": "sexually active", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_activity_status", "concept": "sexually_active", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 5, "technical": 55, "recognition_pct_current": 97, "recognition_pct_peak": 97, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Currently or regularly engaging in sexual intercourse or other sexual activity.", "english_term": "sexually active", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Standard public-health and clinical phrasing, deliberately neutral to avoid stigma. Common in medical history-taking ('are you sexually active?')."}
{"term": "promiscuous", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_promiscuity", "concept": "sexual_promiscuity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 30, "technical": 42, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Having many sexual partners indiscriminately; technically neutral but frequently applied pejoratively, especially to women.", "english_term": "promiscuous", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "The double standard is well-documented: the same behavior in men is often praised as 'virile' while women are shamed as promiscuous. From Latin promiscuus (mixed, indiscriminate)."}
{"term": "loose", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_permissiveness_pejorative", "concept": "sexual_permissiveness_pejorative", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "dated_slang", "crude": 40, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 74, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexually permissive; not constrained by conventional moral standards regarding sex.", "english_term": "loose", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Applied almost exclusively to women; considered dated but still understood. May carry a bodily implication (vaginal looseness after many partners), compounding the shame."}
{"term": "easy", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_permissiveness_pejorative", "concept": "sexual_permissiveness_pejorative", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 35, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Readily willing to engage in sex; sexually permissive (in this sense, always pejorative).", "english_term": "easy", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Heavily polysemous; sexual sense requires context. Applied predominantly to women. Related compounds: 'easy lay', 'easy virtue'. The inversion ('men want someone easy') is culturally notable."}
{"term": "fast", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_permissiveness_pejorative", "concept": "sexual_permissiveness_pejorative", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "dated_slang", "crude": 25, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 42, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexually forward or permissive, especially of a woman; morally lax in sexual matters (dated usage).", "english_term": "fast", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Sexual sense is primarily 19th–mid-20th century; rarely used this way today. Most modern speakers would not parse 'she's fast' as sexual. Peak was pre-living memory."}
{"term": "slutty", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 73, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Behaving or appearing like a slut; sexually provocative or permissive, primarily applied to women as a pejorative.", "english_term": "slutty", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Also applied to clothing and appearance ('slutty Halloween costume'). Some reclamation in queer and feminist communities. Adjectival form of 'slut'; slightly softer in crude score due to adjectival distancing."}
{"term": "whorish", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 80, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 70, "recognition_pct_peak": 76, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Resembling or characteristic of a whore; sexually promiscuous or overtly provocative in a degrading way.", "english_term": "whorish", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Less common than 'slutty'; carries a stronger implication of transactional or mercenary sexuality than mere promiscuity. Adjectival form of 'whore'."}
{"term": "chaste", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "sexual_abstinence", "concept": "sexual_abstinence", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 0, "technical": 22, "recognition_pct_current": 73, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Abstaining from sex outside marriage or from all sexual intercourse; morally pure in a sexual sense.", "english_term": "chaste", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Stronger moral/virtue connotation than 'celibate' or 'abstinent'; implies an internal character quality, not just a behavior. From Latin castus. Usage declining as secularization reduces the moral framework that gives it meaning."}
{"term": "celibate", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "sexual_abstinence", "concept": "sexual_abstinence", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 0, "technical": 48, "recognition_pct_current": 83, "recognition_pct_peak": 83, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Abstaining from marriage and sexual relations, often as a religious vow or deliberate personal commitment.", "english_term": "celibate", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Technically means 'unmarried' (Latin caelibatus) but widely used to mean sexually abstinent. Primarily associated with Catholic clergy. Distinct from 'chaste' (moral purity) and 'abstinent' (behavioral restraint without religious framing)."}
{"term": "abstinent", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "sexual_abstinence", "concept": "sexual_abstinence", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 0, "technical": 55, "recognition_pct_current": 78, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Refraining from sexual activity by deliberate choice, typically for health, moral, or personal reasons.", "english_term": "abstinent", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "More behavioral and secular in connotation than 'chaste' or 'celibate'; used in both sexual and substance-use contexts ('sexually abstinent', 'alcohol-abstinent'). Associated with abstinence-only education discourse."}
{"term": "slut", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 88, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman regarded as sexually promiscuous; one of the most common and loaded misogynistic insults in English.", "english_term": "slut", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Historical sense: 'slovenly woman' (14th c.), sexual sense developed later. Partial reclamation via SlutWalk movement. Occasionally self-applied by women in sexual contexts as subversion. The anchor term for all slut-shame vocabulary."}
{"term": "whore", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 88, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 99, "recognition_pct_peak": 99, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman who has sex for money or is regarded as sexually promiscuous; one of the oldest and most severe sexual pejoratives in English.", "english_term": "whore", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Spans sex work (transactional sense) and slut-shaming (promiscuity sense). Also used as a generic intensifier ('attention whore', 'pizza whore'). Old English hōre; cognate with German Hure, Dutch hoer. Considered more severe than 'slut' in many registers."}
{"term": "hoe", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "subculture", "crude": 72, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A sexually promiscuous woman; AAVE phonetic respelling of 'whore', now widespread in hip-hop and general internet slang.", "english_term": "hoe", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Originated in AAVE; became mainstream via hip-hop from the 1990s onward. The gardening tool homograph creates occasional text ambiguity. Slightly lower crude score than 'whore' due to phonetic distancing and humor register."}
{"term": "ho", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "subculture", "crude": 70, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 84, "recognition_pct_peak": 87, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Variant spelling of 'hoe'; a sexually promiscuous woman (AAVE).", "english_term": "ho", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Same meaning as 'hoe'; this orthography is common in song lyrics, internet text, and rap. Creates comedic collision with Santa Claus's 'ho ho ho'. Slight recognition drop versus 'hoe' because both spellings compete."}
{"term": "thot", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "internet_subculture", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "subculture", "crude": 68, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 67, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": 2015, "regional": null, "definition": "Acronym for 'that hoe over there'; a sexually permissive or attention-seeking woman, used pejoratively in AAVE internet culture.", "english_term": "thot", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Emerged circa 2012 on Twitter and Vine; peaked roughly 2014–2016; declining but still understood. Sometimes pluralized 'thots'. Carries a social-media/performative dimension (seeking attention online) not present in older slut-shame terms."}
{"term": "skank", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 80, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 86, "recognition_pct_peak": 89, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman regarded as sexually promiscuous and typically of low social standing or hygiene; a crude pejorative.", "english_term": "skank", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Uniquely combines sexual shame with class contempt and implied physical uncleanliness — a compound insult. Also a reggae/ska dance move ('skanking'), an entirely unrelated sense."}
{"term": "tramp", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "dated_slang", "crude": 55, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 78, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A sexually permissive woman; a mild pejorative implying loose morals.", "english_term": "tramp", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Also means a homeless vagrant (primary meaning in British English). The sexual sense is primarily American. The Disney film 'Lady and the Tramp' uses the vagabond sense, not the sexual one. Considered dated but still understood."}
{"term": "tart", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 50, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 52, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": "GB", "definition": "A sexually permissive or provocatively dressed woman; a mild British pejorative.", "english_term": "tart", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Primarily British and Australian; American speakers largely know the pastry sense only. Milder than 'slut' in British usage. Folk etymology connects sweetness/availability to pastry, but origin is uncertain."}
{"term": "hussy", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "dated_slang", "crude": 40, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A disreputable, flirtatious, or sexually loose woman; now used primarily in humorous or ironic contexts.", "english_term": "hussy", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Etymologically a corruption of 'housewife'; meaning degraded over several centuries. Largely archaic; often deployed in mock-scolding ('you little hussy!'). One of the milder slut-shame terms."}
{"term": "slag", "lang": "en", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "slut_shame", "concept": "slut_shame", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 82, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 36, "recognition_pct_peak": 55, "peak_year": null, "regional": "GB", "definition": "A sexually promiscuous woman; a crude British pejorative roughly equivalent in force to 'slut'.", "english_term": "slag", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Primarily British and Australian; largely unknown in American English. Primary metallurgical meaning (waste from smelting) is unrelated. Widely used in British soap operas (EastEnders, Coronation Street). Also used as a general insult for men in British English, though the sexual shame connotation is women-coded."}
